\newcommand{\deltamh}{\ensuremath{\Delta_{\text{MH}}}}
\newcommand{\cpct}{\ensuremath{C\%}}

\title{Temporal Measurement Drift in LLM Benchmarks:\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score of 70\% in 2024 reflects the same construct as 70\% in 2023.
We test this assumption using Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, a psychometric method that detects items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability.
Applying Mantel--Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we find that \textbf{31\% of items} exhibit large temporal instability (ETS C classification, $|\deltamh| \geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\%), all with random-split controls below 1\%.
Item-level decomposition shows domain-specific accuracy improvement explains at most 7.6\% of this instability; the signal is predominantly item-specific.
We demonstrate that the field's standard ground truth---web-overlap contamination labels---measures global exposure rather than differential item functioning, yielding enrichment $\approx$1.0 against DIF flags as theoretically expected.
To address this validation gap, we introduce a semi-synthetic framework that injects known differential contamination into real response matrices, with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR\,=\,0.000).
Despite 31\% instability, benchmark rankings remain highly stable ($\rho \approx 0.997$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.
DIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.
\end{abstract}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

When we report that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions.
This assumption of \emph{temporal score comparability} is foundational to leaderboard rankings, model selection decisions, and progress narratives in NLP.
Yet benchmark items are static artifacts deployed in a rapidly evolving ecosystem: training corpora expand, data contamination pathways multiply, and model architectures shift---all of which can alter how individual items function across model generations.

Psychometrics offers a mature framework for testing exactly this assumption: \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability.
An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---signaling that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for at least one group.
The Mantel--Haenszel (MH) procedure, the most widely used DIF method in educational testing, provides both a statistical test and an effect-size measure ($\deltamh$) with well-established classification thresholds.

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix---5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.
Our central finding is that \textbf{31\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across temporal model cohorts (2023 vs.\ 2024-H1), classified as ETS C ($|\deltamh| \geq 1.5$).
This instability generalizes across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\%) and is robust to threshold choice, multiple testing correction, architecture composition, and ability-level confounds.

\paragraph{Instability $\neq$ invalidity.}
A critical interpretive point: DIF flags items whose \emph{functioning changed} across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.
We show that MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\rho \approx 0.997$ after removing all 31\% flagged items), so instability does not threaten ranking validity.
Instead, DIF reveals \emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting across model generations, serving as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
    \item \textbf{Large-scale temporal instability finding}: 31\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF across model generations, confirmed on six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\%), with domain improvement explaining $\leq$7.6\% of the signal.
    \item \textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential functioning. Enrichment $\approx$1.0 against DIF flags is theoretically expected, not a failure. This affects the validity claims of all item-level detectors validated against web-overlap labels.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A $\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR\,=\,0.000) that provides controlled validation for any item-level detector. Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\Delta$TPR\,=\,+0.818).
    \item \textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal ($z$\,=\,$-$8 to $-$49$\sigma$ vs.\ random removal) but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z$\,=\,$-$9.5 vs.\ accuracy-gap $z$\,=\,$-$157). We provide a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintainers.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}
Methods for detecting data contamination in LLMs fall into several categories: membership inference via token-level statistics \citep{shi2024detecting}, perturbation-based approaches that compare performance on original vs.\ rephrased items \citep{oren2024proving}, and overlap-based methods using n-gram matching between training data and benchmarks \citep{li2024task}.
These approaches operate at the model level (testing individual models for contamination) and typically validate against web-overlap labels.
Our work differs fundamentally: DIF operates at the item level across model populations, detecting \emph{which items function differently} rather than \emph{which models are contaminated}.

\paragraph{Benchmark reliability and validity.}
Recent work has questioned benchmark reliability through various lenses: score instability under prompt perturbations \citep{mizrahi2024state}, evaluation framework inconsistencies \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}, and the general ``benchmark lottery'' phenomenon.
Our psychometric approach complements these by providing item-level diagnostics grounded in established measurement theory.

\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}
DIF analysis has a 60-year history in educational and psychological testing \citep{holland1993differential}.
The Mantel--Haenszel procedure \citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988alternate} remains the most widely used method due to its simplicity, robustness, and well-calibrated effect-size classifications (ETS A/B/C).
We apply this established methodology without modification; our contribution is the empirical findings and validation framework, not a new algorithm.

\paragraph{Psychometrics for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM evaluation for efficient testing \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} and characterizing item properties.
Our work extends this psychometric lens to temporal comparability analysis, addressing a question that IRT alone cannot answer: whether items function \emph{equivalently} across model cohorts.

% ============================================================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}
\label{sec:mh-dif}

Given a binary response matrix $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^{N \times K}$ (models $\times$ items), we partition models into a \emph{reference group} (2023 cohort, $N_{\text{ref}}=1{,}080$) and a \emph{focal group} (2024-H1 cohort, $N_{\text{foc}}=807$) based on release date.
For each item $k$, the MH procedure conditions on total score (sum of correct responses across all items) and computes the common odds ratio:
\begin{equation}
    \alpha_{\text{MH},k} = \frac{\sum_j A_j D_j / T_j}{\sum_j B_j C_j / T_j}
\end{equation}
where $A_j, B_j, C_j, D_j$ are the entries of the $2\times 2$ table at score stratum $j$, and $T_j$ is the stratum total.
The log-odds effect size on the ETS delta scale is $\deltamh = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_{\text{MH}})$.

Items are classified using the ETS system: \textbf{A} (negligible, $|\deltamh| < 1.0$), \textbf{B} (moderate, $1.0 \leq |\deltamh| < 1.5$), and \textbf{C} (large, $|\deltamh| \geq 1.5$).
C-classified items are our primary measure of temporal instability.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}
\label{sec:semi-synthetic}

To validate DIF detection power with known ground truth, we inject synthetic differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
    \item Select 500 clean baseline items and 200 target items for injection.
    \item For each target item, flip incorrect$\to$correct responses in the focal group with exploitation rate $p \in \{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$.
    \item Run MH-DIF and measure True Positive Rate (TPR) and False Positive Rate (FPR).
    \item Report $\Delta$TPR = TPR($p$) $-$ TPR($p$=0) as the detection power increase attributable to injected contamination.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}
\label{sec:cross-subject}

Standard MH-DIF conditions on total score computed over all items including the tested item.
When contamination inflates scores on tested items, this \emph{matching variable contamination} biases the conditioning, inflating FPR.
We propose \textbf{cross-subject matching}: for items in subject $s$, compute the matching variable as total score on all subjects \emph{except} $s$.
This eliminates the feedback loop between contaminated items and the matching variable.

% ============================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

All analyses use the METABENCH response matrix.
Our primary analysis focuses on MMLU (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subjects), with cross-benchmark replication on five additional benchmarks.

\subsection{Temporal DIF: 31\% Instability Across Six Benchmarks}
\label{sec:temporal-dif}

\paragraph{MMLU results.}
Table~\ref{tab:temporal-dif} presents the core temporal DIF analysis.
Between the 2023 reference cohort ($N$=1,080) and 2024-H1 focal cohort ($N$=807), 31.3\% of MMLU items are classified as ETS C (large DIF), with 16.3\% as B (moderate) and 52.4\% as A (negligible).
The random-split sanity check yields only 0.18\% C-classified items, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.
The ability-quintile split (high vs.\ low ability, same time period) produces only 2.9\% C items, demonstrating that DIF captures temporal rather than ability-driven differences.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Temporal DIF summary (ETS classification). C\% is the primary instability measure ($|\deltamh| \geq 1.5$). Random split serves as null control.}
\label{tab:temporal-dif}
\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Split} & $N_{\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\text{foc}}$ & \textbf{Items} & \textbf{A\%} & \textbf{B\%} & \textbf{C\%} & \textbf{Rand.\ C\%} \\
\midrule
Temporal & 1,080 & 807 & 12,508 & 52.4 & 16.3 & 31.3 & 0.18 \\
Ability quintile & 1,044 & 1,044 & 12,508 & 85.2 & 11.9 & 2.9 & 0.15 \\
Within-family (Llama) & 161 & 308 & 12,508 & 24.1 & 14.6 & 61.3 & 0.22 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark generalization.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross-benchmark} shows temporal DIF across all six METABENCH benchmarks.
Every benchmark exhibits substantial instability (17.9--31.3\% C), with all random controls below 1\%.
Multi-domain benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA) show higher C\% (29.7--31.3\%) than single-domain benchmarks (GSM8K 18.9\%, HellaSwag 17.9\%), consistent with greater item heterogeneity providing more opportunities for differential functioning.
The minimum enrichment ratio versus random is 45$\times$, confirming temporal DIF far exceeds chance across all benchmarks.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. All random controls are below 1\%. Ratio = C\% / Random C\%.}
\label{tab:cross-benchmark}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
