\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Domain specificity.}
C\% varies across MMLU subjects from 20.7\% (computer security) to 48.4\% (business ethics), with Spearman $\rho$\,=\,0.307 ($p$\,=\,0.02) between subject-level C\% and accuracy improvement.
However, item-level decomposition (\S\ref{sec:confounds}) shows this modest ecological correlation overstates the actual contribution.

\subsection{Ruling Out Confounds}
\label{sec:confounds}

\paragraph{Domain improvement decomposition.}
Table~\ref{tab:regression} presents logistic regression models predicting DIF flag status from accuracy change and item properties.
Model 1 (subject-level accuracy change only) yields pseudo $R^2$\,=\,0.0007---domain improvement explains essentially none of the DIF variance.
The odds ratio of 1.55 per 0.10 accuracy increase reverses to 0.73 in Model 2 when item properties (difficulty, discrimination) are added, exhibiting Simpson's paradox: the apparent domain-level association vanishes once item-level properties are controlled.
Item-level accuracy change (Model 3) produces similarly negligible explanatory power ($R^2$\,=\,0.0012).
CTT discrimination emerges as the strongest predictor (OR\,=\,1.54 per 0.10 unit), indicating high-discrimination items are more likely to be flagged---consistent with DIF theory, as these items are more sensitive to any cohort composition differences.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Logistic regression predicting DIF C flag. OR = odds ratio per 0.10 unit increase.}
\label{tab:regression}
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Predictors} & \textbf{McFadden $R^2$} & \textbf{Nagelkerke $R^2$} & \textbf{Key OR} \\
\midrule
M1 & subject acc.\ $\Delta$ & 0.0007 & 0.0012 & 1.55 [1.19, 2.03] \\
M2 & + item properties & 0.0423 & 0.072 & 0.73 (reversed) \\
M3 & item acc.\ $\Delta$ & 0.0012 & 0.0021 & 1.08 [1.51, 2.96] \\
M4 & + item properties & 0.0434 & 0.074 & 0.92 (reversed) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}
METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).
Excluding the single non-decoder-only model produces identical C\% (31.3\%) with Jaccard\,=\,1.0 against the full-population analysis, confirming architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue.

\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}
\label{sec:meta-critique}

Existing contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels---binary indicators of whether benchmark items appear in web-crawled training data.
We argue these labels measure \emph{global exposure} (an item is publicly accessible), not \emph{differential contamination} (an item functions differently across model cohorts).
These are orthogonal constructs: an item can be web-exposed yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or be non-exposed yet function differently due to indirect learning pathways.

We test this directly: DIF enrichment against Li et al.\ web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items yields enrichment\,=\,0.964 (Jaccard\,=\,0.206, $\phi$\,=\,0.012, $\chi^2$\,=\,1.28, $p$\,=\,0.258).
This near-unity enrichment is \emph{expected}, not a failure---it confirms the theoretical orthogonality.
The implication is field-wide: any item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap labels (including Min-K\%, ConStat, n-gram methods) has this blind spot.
Semi-synthetic validation (\S\ref{sec:semi-synthetic-results}) provides a controlled alternative.

