\paragraph{DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter.}
DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal: removing DIF-flagged items causes significantly more ranking disruption than random removal ($z$\,=\,$-$8 to $-$49$\sigma$ across 21 conditions).
However, circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\times$ more ($z$\,=\,$-$157), high-variance removal 11$\times$ more ($z$\,=\,$-$108), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\times$ more ($z$\,=\,$-$16).
The MH procedure's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.
DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\% (Jaccard\,=\,0.12)---DIF identifies ``items that changed across generations,'' not ``good measurement items.''

% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Implications for benchmark maintenance.}
Our findings suggest a practical audit protocol for benchmark maintainers.
Items with persistent C-level DIF across multiple cohort definitions warrant investigation---not automatic removal, but diagnostic attention.
The graceful degradation results (\S\ref{sec:downstream}) show that targeted replacement is safe: even replacing all 31\% flagged items preserves ranking validity ($\rho$\,=\,0.990).
The massive measurement redundancy in MMLU (12,508 items, $\rho$\,$\approx$\,0.997 under 31\% removal) provides a large safety margin for item rotation.

\paragraph{What drives the instability?}
DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d$\,=\,$-$0.247) and higher-discrimination ($d$\,=\,0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across domains ($\chi^2$ $p$\,=\,0.32).
A striking difficulty $\times$ direction interaction ($\rho$\,=\,$-$0.597) reveals a systematic pattern: hardest-decile DIF items are 88.7\% focal-favoring (2024 models perform better), while easiest-decile items are 96.2\% reference-favoring (2023 models perform better).
This suggests capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.

\paragraph{Complementarity with existing approaches.}
DIF and contamination detectors (Min-K\%, ConStat) address different questions.
Contamination detectors ask ``has this model seen this item?'' (model-level, individual diagnosis).
DIF asks ``does this item function differently across model generations?'' (item-level, population diagnosis).
The web-overlap enrichment $\approx$1.0 confirms these are orthogonal signals, not competing methods.
Benchmark maintainers should use both: DIF for population-level item auditing, contamination detectors for individual model vetting.

% ============================================================
\section*{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
    \item \textbf{Unidimensional IRT misspecification.} MH-DIF assumes unidimensional ability, but MMLU spans 57 subjects. Bifactor analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:bifactor}) confirms the general factor explains only 31\% of variance. Future work should explore multidimensional IRT and MIMIC models for more principled conditioning.
    \item \textbf{Semi-synthetic simplification.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\to$correct flipping) models one contamination pathway. Real contamination may produce subtler response pattern changes. The framework's value lies in providing \emph{any} controlled ground truth where none existed before.
    \item \textbf{Temporal cohort definition.} Our 2023/2024-H1 binary split is a simplification; model release dates are noisy proxies for training data cutoffs. Item-Focused Trees analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:ift}) using continuous release dates yields similar results (34\% detection rate, Jaccard\,=\,0.81 with MH).
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing that 31\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations---a finding that generalizes across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\%).
This instability is predominantly item-specific (domain improvement explains $\leq$7.6\%), does not threaten ranking validity ($\rho$\,$\approx$\,0.997), and is invisible to the field's standard web-overlap ground truth (enrichment\,$\approx$\,1.0).
Our semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\Delta$FPR\,=\,0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detection method.
DIF serves as a diagnostic lens---revealing what changed across model generations---not a quality filter, with direct implications for benchmark maintenance practices.

